{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cf2qnkhmPDp29UEdsJ7uS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BjChYR943ERCRxpLKET7TR","name":"Skill","input":{"skill":"auto-claude-skills:synthesize","args":"Synthesize the two independent Claude-family panelist plans above (Opus 5 and Sonnet 5) for adding per-tenant rate limiting to an existing Python HTTP service with no rate limiting today, into a single implementation plan to follow. Both plans converged on: GCRA/token-bucket algorithm implemented as an atomic Redis Lua script; Redis (not in-process/DB) as the backend with TTL'd keys and fail-open behavior on Redis errors; pure ASGI (or equivalent) middleware positioned after auth-resolves-tenant and before routing; tenant_id-derived key (not client-supplied header) with separate limit classes/buckets per endpoint cost tier; config split into static deploy-time tier defaults plus dynamic per-tenant overrides with a global kill switch; RateLimit-*/Retry-After headers and 429 + problem-json responses; metrics without tenant_id as a label (cardinality), structured logs for throttle events, alerting on fail-open and anomalous throttle spikes; unit tests on the algorithm with injected clock, integration tests on Lua atomicity under concurrency and middleware behavior including Redis-down fail-open; and a phased shadow-mode -> internal-enforce -> tiered-canary rollout with a documented rollback switch. Opus's plan additionally covers: cost-weighted requests (settling actual cost post-handler), IETF RateLimit-* headers plus RFC9457 problem+json body, hash-tagged Redis Cluster keys, circuit breaker after repeated Redis failures, XFF-spoofing risk for unauthenticated IP-keyed limits, a secondary in-process concurrency-limiter safety valve, and an explicit recommendation to prefer edge/gateway rate limiting over in-app when tenant identity/cost isn't auth-dependent. Produce one prioritized, concrete implementation plan (with phased milestones and the key open questions to confirm against the real codebase, e.g. framework, existing Redis availability, presence of an edge gateway) that a developer can execute directly, resolving any differences between the two plans with a stated rationale rather than listing both options."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14782,"cache_read_input_tokens":23978,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14782},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8506c09a-43c3-4d2f-8a21-0ab145d0847c","uuid":"a1d4a274-0404-4c76-9ecb-056169ccc7c4","timestamp":"2026-09-14T06:16:08.362Z","request_id":"req_011Cf2qnkBmHqYW99dtXkytT"}
